Subject: compiler, api: register generated code with perf through jitdump

Wasmer could name generated code for perf with a perf map, but only in a
limited way: a compiler setting turned it on, so a headless engine
loading artifacts never wrote one; it listed only functions with a
name-section entry and no trampolines; and a forked child, which has a
new PID, had no map at all. A perf map also gives perf nothing to unwind
with. perf's DWARF unwinder needs each function's unwind information and
code, so a call graph that reached generated code stopped there.

An engine now carries a ProfileMode, Off, PerfMap or JitDump, chosen
with EngineBuilder::set_profile_mode, headless engines included; a
compiler with perf maps enabled still selects PerfMap. The engine
registers every function and trampoline once its code is executable and
its runtime unwind information is published, before either artifact
format's load hands the artifact back. A local function is named by its
name-section entry, else by the least of its export names, else as
wasm[N] with its full function index; the trampolines are
wasmer::call_trampoline[N] and wasmer::dynamic_trampoline[N]. A native
ELF artifact's extents come from its symbol table, so profiling one that
has none fails to load.

JitDump writes perf's jitdump format. Each process gets one dump,
jit-<pid>.dump, in a new /tmp/jitted-wasmer-* directory, since perf's
libdw unwinder recognises generated code only under /tmp/jitted- and a
directory of its own keeps a reused PID from overwriting a recording.
The dump's header page is mapped executable as the marker perf looks
for, and its timestamps are CLOCK_MONOTONIC, to be recorded with -k 1.
Every region is an unwinding-information record followed by a code-load
record with the region's code. The unwinding information is a
self-contained .eh_frame and .eh_frame_hdr for that one region, converted
from the module's published .eh_frame: its CIE and FDE rules are copied,
the personality and LSDA a profiler never uses are dropped, and its
addresses are rebased to the layout of the ELF file perf inject builds
for the region. A process-wide lock keeps each registration's records
together across engines and threads, code indexes are unique within the
process, a close record is written at exit, and a failed write stops
all later output rather than extend a stream it may have corrupted.

A registration keeps a copy of its regions, with their code for a
jitdump, for as long as its engine keeps the code allocated; the process
list refers to it weakly and drops expired entries as it grows.
profile_after_fork, called in a forked child before it runs inherited
code or starts a thread, gives the child its own dump or map and replays
every live registration into it; a later call in the same process does
nothing, and a registration attempted in a child before it is refused.
It is unsafe because it relies on loading, profiling and engine
destruction having been quiescent at the fork. A perf map uses the same
names and regions, appends each registration's lines in one write and is
replayed the same way. Hosts other than Linux still get perf maps;
jitdump is Linux-only.

Unit tests cover jitdump record sizes, timestamps and unique indexes,
refusing an exhausted index or an oversized record, a failed write
stopping later output, converted unwind information keeping each
region's own rules under shared CIEs and both address encodings while
dropping personality and LSDA references and refusing a duplicate FDE,
a region's search header leading gimli's reader to its table and rules
at the addresses perf gives them, and name selection.

